[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Auto Indexing in Oracle Database"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Auto Indexing is a feature in Oracle Database designed to automatically create indexes on tables to improve query performance. It analyzes workload patterns and identifies potential indexes that could benefit the database. This feature aims to reduce the manual effort required for index creation and maintenance, while optimizing query execution."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Enabling Auto Indexing"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Auto Indexing is not enabled by default. You need to explicitly enable it using the `DBMS_AUTO_INDEX` package. The following steps outline the process:"},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"BEGIN
  DBMS_AUTO_INDEX.CONFIGURE('AUTO_INDEX_MODE', 'ON');
END;
/"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"This PL/SQL block configures the `AUTO_INDEX_MODE` parameter to 'ON', which enables the auto indexing feature. You can also set it to 'REPORT ONLY' to only generate reports without creating indexes."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Configuring Auto Indexing Parameters"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Several parameters can be configured to control the behavior of Auto Indexing. Some important parameters include:"},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`AUTO_INDEX_MODE`: Specifies whether auto indexing is enabled ('ON'), disabled ('OFF'), or in report-only mode ('REPORT ONLY')."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`AUTO_INDEX_SCHEMA`: Specifies the schema(s) for which auto indexing should be considered. By default, it applies to all schemas."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`AUTO_INDEX_REPORT_RETENTION`: Specifies how long auto indexing reports are retained."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`AUTO_INDEX_DEFAULT_TABLESPACE`: Specifies the default tablespace where auto-created indexes will be stored."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"BEGIN
  DBMS_AUTO_INDEX.CONFIGURE('AUTO_INDEX_SCHEMA', 'HR,OE');
  DBMS_AUTO_INDEX.CONFIGURE('AUTO_INDEX_DEFAULT_TABLESPACE', 'USERS');
END;
/"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"This example configures auto indexing to only consider the 'HR' and 'OE' schemas and sets the default tablespace for auto-created indexes to 'USERS'."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Monitoring Auto Indexing"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can monitor the activity of Auto Indexing using the `DBA_AUTO_INDEX_EXECUTIONS` and `DBA_AUTO_INDEX_PLANS` views. These views provide information about the indexes that have been created, considered, or rejected by the auto indexing process."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"SELECT execution_name, status, start_time, end_time
FROM DBA_AUTO_INDEX_EXECUTIONS;

SELECT table_name, index_name, plan_status, reason
FROM DBA_AUTO_INDEX_PLANS;
/"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"These queries allow you to view the execution history and the plans generated by the auto indexing process, including the status and reasons for index creation or rejection."},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Disabling Auto Indexing"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"To disable Auto Indexing, you can set the `AUTO_INDEX_MODE` parameter to 'OFF':"},{"order":21,"type":"code","text":"BEGIN
  DBMS_AUTO_INDEX.CONFIGURE('AUTO_INDEX_MODE', 'OFF');
END;
/"},{"order":22,"type":"subheading","text":"Considerations and Best Practices"},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Carefully monitor the indexes created by Auto Indexing to ensure they are beneficial and do not negatively impact performance."},{"order":24,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Consider setting the `AUTO_INDEX_SCHEMA` parameter to limit the scope of Auto Indexing to specific schemas."},{"order":25,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Regularly review the `DBA_AUTO_INDEX_PLANS` view to understand the decisions made by the Auto Indexing process."},{"order":26,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Test Auto Indexing in a non-production environment before enabling it in production."},{"order":27,"type":"paragraph","text":"Auto Indexing can be a valuable tool for optimizing query performance, but it's essential to understand its behavior and monitor its impact on the database."}]
